-- Q1440: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The federal program name, Assistance Listing number, awarding agency, and expenditure amount for each program","Only the total amount of federal awards received during the fiscal year","Only major programs with expenditures exceeding the Type A threshold","A listing of all state and local grants received during the period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.510), the SEFA must include the federal program name, Assistance Listing (formerly CFDA) number, the federal awarding agency, and the amount expended for each program. It covers all federal awards, not only major programs.
Wrong (B): The SEFA requires itemized detail by program, not merely a single aggregate total of all federal awards received.
Wrong (C): All federal programs must be reported on the SEFA regardless of expenditure amount, not only those exceeding the Type A threshold.
Wrong (D): The SEFA reports federal awards only; state and local grants are outside its scope.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1440;

-- Q1441: Audit Planning [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To communicate the detailed audit approach to the client''s audit committee","To evaluate the results of substantive testing procedures performed","To document the specific audit procedures for each account balance","To set the scope, timing, and direction of the audit and guide planning"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 300.07, the overall audit strategy sets the scope, timing, and direction of the audit and guides development of the detailed audit plan. It addresses resource allocation, timing of deployment, and how work is managed and supervised.
Wrong (A): Communicating with the audit committee is a separate requirement under AU-C 260, not the primary purpose of the overall audit strategy.
Wrong (B): Evaluating substantive testing results occurs during fieldwork and reporting, not during the strategy-setting phase.
Wrong (C): Documenting specific procedures for each account is the function of the detailed audit plan under AU-C 300.09, not the overall strategy.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1441;

-- Q1447: Audit Planning [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The engagement letter level as specified in the agreement","The financial statement level as a single overall threshold","The management representation letter level for assertions","The individual account balance or class of transactions level"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 320.A12, tolerable misstatement is applied at the individual account balance or class of transactions level. It represents performance materiality applied to a particular sampling procedure and helps determine sample sizes under AU-C 530.
Wrong (A): The engagement letter documents engagement terms under AU-C 210 but is not a level at which materiality is applied.
Wrong (B): Overall materiality under AU-C 320.04 is set at the financial statement level; tolerable misstatement operates below that threshold at the account level.
Wrong (C): The management representation letter is a communication tool under AU-C 580, not a materiality allocation level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1447;

-- Q1450: Audit Planning [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Establish the audit fee based on the scope of internal audit coverage","Evaluate whether the entity actually needs an internal audit function","Replace external audit procedures entirely with internal audit work","Determine whether internal audit work can modify planned audit procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 610.09, the auditor evaluates the internal audit function during planning to determine whether its work can be used to modify the nature, timing, or extent of external audit procedures. This assessment considers competence, objectivity, and systematic approach under AU-C 610.12.
Wrong (A): Audit fees are determined by engagement economics, not by the extent of internal audit coverage available.
Wrong (B): Advising on whether the entity needs an internal audit function is a management decision outside the external auditor''s role.
Wrong (C): AU-C 610.05 confirms the external auditor retains sole responsibility for the opinion and cannot replace procedures entirely with internal audit work.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1450;

-- Q1454: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Determining the audit fees for the current year engagement","Assessing the risks of material misstatement and planning audit procedures","Deciding whether to withdraw from the current audit engagement","Preparing the engagement letter for the following year''s audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315.12, a change in revenue recognition policy directly impacts risk assessment and planned audit procedures. AU-C 315.A60 requires the auditor to evaluate whether the change is appropriate, properly applied, and adequately disclosed under the applicable framework.
Wrong (A): Audit fee determination is an administrative matter separate from the auditor''s responsibility to assess risks arising from accounting policy changes.
Wrong (C): A change in accounting policy alone does not constitute grounds for withdrawal; the auditor should assess and respond to the associated risks.
Wrong (D): Preparing next year''s engagement letter is a future administrative task unrelated to the current-year risk implications of the policy change.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1454;

-- Q1463: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Obtain the predecessor''s complete set of working papers","Determine the audit fee that the predecessor charged the client","Obtain information bearing on acceptance, such as management disagreements","Transfer responsibility for the prior year audit to the successor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 210 and AU-C 510, the successor auditor communicates with the predecessor to obtain information bearing on the acceptance decision, including reasons for the change, disagreements with management, and concerns about management integrity. This communication requires client permission.
Wrong (A): The primary purpose is to gather acceptance-relevant information, not to obtain the full set of working papers; access to work papers is a separate, subsequent step.
Wrong (B): The predecessor''s fee is irrelevant to evaluating the risks associated with accepting the engagement.
Wrong (D): Responsibility for the prior year audit remains with the predecessor; it cannot be transferred to the successor through pre-acceptance communication.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1463;

-- Q1465: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["GAAP prohibits all related party transactions between affiliated entities","The auditor must pre-approve all related party transactions before execution","Related party transactions are presumed to be fraudulent in all cases","Related party transactions may lack arm''s-length terms and cause misstatement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 550.06, the auditor considers related party relationships primarily because such transactions may not be conducted at arm''s-length terms, which could result in material misstatement or inadequate disclosure. AU-C 550.A10 notes economic substance may differ from legal form.
Wrong (A): GAAP does not prohibit related party transactions; it requires proper disclosure and consideration of their substance under ASC 850.
Wrong (B): The auditor has no authority or responsibility to approve an entity''s transactions; the auditor''s role is to assess risk and evaluate disclosure.
Wrong (C): Related party transactions are not presumed fraudulent. The concern is that non-arm''s-length terms may create misstatement risk, not that fraud necessarily exists.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1465;

-- Q1469: Audit Planning [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Materiality can only be increased, never decreased, during the audit","Materiality revisions require prior approval from the client''s management","The auditor should revise materiality when new information warrants it","Materiality is fixed during planning and cannot be changed afterward"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 320.12, the auditor should revise materiality for the financial statements as a whole if information comes to the auditor''s attention during the audit that would have caused a different amount to be established initially. Materiality may be revised upward or downward.
Wrong (A): AU-C 320 permits materiality to be revised in either direction—both increases and decreases—based on new information obtained during the audit.
Wrong (B): Materiality is an auditor judgment under AU-C 320; it does not require client approval since it is a tool for planning and evaluating misstatements.
Wrong (D): AU-C 320.12 explicitly requires the auditor to reconsider materiality when circumstances change, so it is not fixed at planning.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1469;

-- Q1470: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Perform procedures on the internal auditors'' work to evaluate its quality","Reperform every procedure the internal auditor originally completed","Obtain the internal auditor''s professional license number on file","Issue a joint audit report together with the internal audit function"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 610.12–.14, when the external auditor uses internal auditors'' work as audit evidence, the external auditor must perform sufficient procedures on that body of work to evaluate its quality and effectiveness. This includes examining tested items and reperforming certain procedures.
Wrong (B): Reperforming all of the internal auditor''s work is unnecessary and impractical; AU-C 610 requires evaluation of the work, not wholesale duplication.
Wrong (C): A professional license number is not a required element of the external auditor''s evaluation; the assessment focuses on competence, objectivity, and work quality.
Wrong (D): The external auditor retains sole responsibility for the audit opinion under AU-C 610.05 and does not issue joint reports with internal audit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1470;

-- Q1471: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Refuse to audit the investment property account entirely","Hire a separate specialist to redo the entire valuation independently","Accept the specialist''s work as audit evidence without further evaluation","Evaluate the specialist''s competence, objectivity, and work appropriateness"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 500.08, when management uses a specialist, the auditor must evaluate the specialist''s competence, capabilities, and objectivity, and assess whether the work is appropriate as audit evidence. The auditor considers the data sources, assumptions, and methods employed.
Wrong (A): Refusing to audit the account is inappropriate; the auditor should evaluate the specialist''s work and determine whether it provides sufficient evidence.
Wrong (B): Hiring a separate specialist to redo the entire valuation is not required; the auditor may evaluate management''s specialist rather than commissioning duplicate work.
Wrong (C): Accepting specialist work without evaluation violates AU-C 500.08, which requires the auditor to assess the relevance and reliability of all audit evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1471;

-- Q1476: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Materiality applies only to the balance sheet, not other statements","Materiality levels for different statements require audit committee approval","The same materiality level must be applied uniformly across all statements","Different materiality levels may be set for different financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 320.10, the auditor may determine that different materiality levels are appropriate for different financial statements. Users may be more sensitive to income statement misstatements, warranting a lower threshold for those items. The auditor uses the lowest applicable materiality to drive procedures.
Wrong (A): Materiality applies to all financial statements, including the income statement and cash flow statement, not just the balance sheet.
Wrong (B): Setting materiality is an auditor judgment under AU-C 320; it does not require audit committee approval, though the auditor may communicate the approach.
Wrong (C): AU-C 320.10 explicitly permits different materiality levels for different statements when circumstances warrant, so a uniform level is not required.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1476;

-- Q1478: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept but use alternative procedures to obtain predecessor information","Decline the engagement because predecessor communication is mandatory","Accept without additional procedures since predecessor inquiry is optional","Report the predecessor firm to the state board of accountancy"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 210 and AU-C 510, while communication with the predecessor is ordinarily required, circumstances such as firm dissolution may prevent it. The successor auditor should consider alternative procedures—reviewing prior year financial statements, SEC filings, or regulatory reports—to obtain the needed information.
Wrong (B): AU-C 510 does not mandate declining the engagement when predecessor communication is impossible; it requires the auditor to consider alternative means of obtaining relevant information.
Wrong (C): Accepting without additional procedures ignores the risk assessment purpose of predecessor communication and could leave the auditor without sufficient information for planning.
Wrong (D): Reporting the dissolved firm to a state board serves no audit planning purpose and is not required by professional standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1478;

-- Q1479: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Map the structure, identify intercompany transactions, and assess elimination risk","Accept management''s consolidation schedules without independent testing","Audit only the parent company''s standalone financial statements","Reduce materiality to zero for all intercompany transaction balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 600 and AU-C 315, complex organizational structures require the auditor to map the entity''s structure, identify significant intercompany balances and transactions, and assess the risk that elimination entries may be misstated or incomplete. Improper eliminations can materially misstate consolidated statements.
Wrong (B): Accepting management''s consolidation schedules without testing fails to provide sufficient appropriate audit evidence under AU-C 500 for a high-risk area.
Wrong (C): Auditing only the parent''s standalone statements ignores the consolidated reporting obligation and the risks inherent in intercompany transactions.
Wrong (D): Reducing materiality to zero is impractical and not required; the auditor should apply professional judgment to set appropriate materiality for intercompany balances.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1479;

-- Q1480: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assess fraud risk implications and evaluate anti-corruption compliance programs","Ignore jurisdiction-specific risks if the entity has a code of ethics","Limit the audit scope to only the entity''s domestic operations","Refuse the engagement because the jurisdictional risk is too high"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 240.A16 and AU-C 315, operations in high-corruption jurisdictions require the auditor to consider jurisdiction-specific fraud risks. This includes evaluating management''s anti-corruption compliance programs and planning procedures to address FCPA or similar regulatory requirements.
Wrong (B): A code of ethics alone does not mitigate jurisdiction-specific corruption risks; the auditor must still assess the operating environment and compliance controls.
Wrong (C): Limiting the audit to domestic operations would leave significant foreign operations untested, failing to obtain sufficient evidence under AU-C 500.
Wrong (D): High jurisdictional risk alone does not require declining the engagement; the auditor should assess whether adequate procedures can be designed to address the risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1480;

-- Q1481: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Dismiss the concern because revenue recognition is always a presumed risk","Evaluate the concern against specific facts and determine the audit response","Remove the associate from the engagement for questioning the judgment","Immediately issue a going concern opinion on the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 240.27 and AU-C 315, while revenue recognition is a presumed fraud risk, the specific complexity of multiple performance obligations warrants separate evaluation as a significant risk. The engagement partner should evaluate the facts and determine whether additional procedures are needed beyond the standard fraud risk response.
Wrong (A): Dismissing the concern ignores that specific revenue complexities may require additional procedures beyond the presumed fraud risk response under AU-C 240.
Wrong (C): Removing a team member for raising a legitimate audit concern would violate professional standards emphasizing open communication within the engagement team under AU-C 220.
Wrong (D): A going concern opinion relates to the entity''s ability to continue operating, not to the complexity of revenue recognition arrangements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1481;

-- Q1482: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The decision to change the entity''s financial reporting framework","The requirement to report the restatement to regulatory authorities","The decision to set the audit fee higher than the prior year","The risk assessment and planned procedures for inventory valuation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.11, a prior year restatement due to inventory valuation errors directly impacts the auditor''s current-year risk assessment. The auditor would likely assess a higher risk of material misstatement for inventory, plan more extensive substantive procedures, and evaluate whether the underlying cause has been remediated.
Wrong (A): A restatement due to an error does not require changing the financial reporting framework; it indicates misapplication under the existing framework.
Wrong (B): Reporting to regulators may be required in some circumstances, but it is not the most direct impact on the auditor''s planning responsibilities.
Wrong (C): Audit fee determination is an administrative matter separate from the auditor''s professional obligation to assess and respond to identified risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1482;

-- Q1483: Audit Planning [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Time zone differences between the various component locations","Language differences affecting the audit documentation and reports","The cost of travel to geographically dispersed component locations","Differing reporting frameworks, auditing standards, and regulatory rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 600.10, the most significant challenge in multi-jurisdiction group audits involves differences in financial reporting frameworks, auditing standards, and regulatory requirements. These can affect the nature and extent of component auditors'' work and the need for reconciling procedures.
Wrong (A): Time zone differences are logistical challenges but do not substantively affect the quality or nature of audit procedures performed.
Wrong (B): Language differences may complicate documentation but are less significant than fundamental differences in the frameworks and standards governing the audit.
Wrong (C): Travel costs are administrative considerations that do not directly affect the auditor''s assessment of audit quality or the nature of procedures required.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1483;

-- Q1484: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The realizability of deferred tax assets, including future income sufficiency","Accepting management''s assertion that deferred tax assets are fully realizable","Requiring the company to write off all deferred tax assets before acceptance","Ignoring deferred tax assets because they represent non-cash balance items"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 540 and ASC 740, deferred tax assets require careful evaluation of realizability, which involves significant judgment. The auditor must plan procedures to assess whether sufficient future taxable income exists, evaluate the nature of temporary differences, and determine whether a valuation allowance is needed.
Wrong (B): Accepting management''s assertion without independent evaluation violates AU-C 500, which requires the auditor to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence rather than rely solely on management representations.
Wrong (C): Requiring write-off as a condition of acceptance exceeds the auditor''s role; the auditor evaluates the appropriateness of the recorded amount, not dictates accounting treatment.
Wrong (D): Deferred tax assets are significant balance sheet items subject to estimation uncertainty and cannot be excluded from audit scope simply because they are non-cash.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1484;

-- Q1485: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Understand the claim, evaluate management''s assessment, and inquire of legal counsel","Ignore the lawsuit because all litigation outcomes are inherently uncertain","Immediately include a going concern paragraph in the audit report","Advise the client to settle the lawsuit before the audit field work begins"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 501.09 and AU-C 337, significant pending litigation requires the auditor to understand the claim, evaluate management''s assessment of probable outcomes, and send a letter of audit inquiry to the entity''s legal counsel. The auditor also evaluates whether proper accrual and disclosure have been made under ASC 450.
Wrong (B): Litigation uncertainty does not excuse the auditor from evaluating potential contingent liabilities; AU-C 501 requires active assessment of claims and possible losses.
Wrong (C): Including a going concern paragraph requires evidence that the entity may not continue as a going concern under AU-C 570, which is a separate evaluation from loss contingencies.
Wrong (D): Advising the client on legal strategy is outside the auditor''s professional role; the auditor assesses the financial statement implications of existing litigation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1485;

-- Q1486: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the engagement since predecessor communication is merely a courtesy","Report the prospective client to the appropriate regulatory authorities","Accept the engagement but plan to issue a qualified audit opinion","Treat the refusal as a factor affecting the acceptance decision"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 210.10 and AU-C 510, a client''s refusal to permit predecessor communication is a scope limitation that should be carefully evaluated. The auditor should consider the implications for management integrity and whether this restriction affects the ability to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence.
Wrong (A): Predecessor communication is a professional requirement under AU-C 210, not a courtesy; treating it as optional understates its importance in the acceptance decision.
Wrong (B): A client''s refusal to authorize predecessor communication does not trigger a regulatory reporting obligation on the part of the prospective auditor.
Wrong (C): The decision to accept or decline precedes the audit itself; planning to issue a qualified opinion before starting fieldwork is premature and not a proper response to a pre-acceptance limitation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1486;

-- Q1487: Audit Planning [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the going concern assessment for the restructured entity","Only the inventory valuation at restructured plant locations","Only the staffing plan for the current audit engagement team","Risk assessment across multiple areas including impairment and disclosures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315 and AU-C 540, major restructuring affects multiple financial statement areas and requires comprehensive risk assessment. The auditor must evaluate asset impairment, restructuring charge estimates, employee severance obligations, lease termination costs, and disclosure adequacy. Limiting the assessment to one area would be insufficient.
Wrong (A): While going concern is relevant, focusing only on that assessment ignores other affected areas such as asset impairment, severance liabilities, and restructuring charges.
Wrong (B): Inventory valuation is only one of many areas affected by restructuring; the auditor must also consider impairments, benefit obligations, and lease terminations.
Wrong (C): The audit staffing plan is an administrative consideration, not a financial statement area affected by the entity''s restructuring activities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1487;

-- Q1498: Risk Assessment [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Relates pervasively to the financial statements and may affect many assertions","Relates specifically to a single account balance or class of transactions","Can be fully addressed through a single well-designed substantive test","Is always caused by fraudulent activity within the organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.A105, financial statement-level risks relate pervasively to the financial statements as a whole and potentially affect many assertions. Examples include a weak control environment or management integrity concerns. AU-C 330.05 requires overall responses such as assigning more experienced staff.
Wrong (B): Risks that relate to a single account balance or transaction class are assertion-level risks, not financial statement-level risks under AU-C 315.
Wrong (C): Pervasive financial statement-level risks require overall responses affecting the entire audit approach, not a single substantive procedure.
Wrong (D): Financial statement-level risks can arise from errors, weak controls, or environmental factors, not exclusively from fraud.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1498;

-- Q1500: Risk Assessment [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Relying entirely on the entity''s internal controls over valuation","Issuing a disclaimer of opinion on the financial statements","Decreasing the extent of planned substantive audit procedures","Designing more effective substantive procedures or testing closer to year-end"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330.07, when inherent risk is assessed as high for a particular assertion, the auditor must design more effective substantive procedures to reduce detection risk. This may include testing closer to period-end, using larger samples, or employing more reliable evidence types such as external confirmations under AU-C 505.
Wrong (A): Relying entirely on controls is inappropriate when inherent risk is high; substantive procedures are required to address the elevated risk.
Wrong (B): A disclaimer of opinion is issued when the auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence, not as a routine response to high inherent risk.
Wrong (C): Decreasing substantive procedures when inherent risk is high would increase detection risk beyond acceptable levels under AU-C 200.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1500;

-- Q1503: Risk Assessment [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assign more experienced staff, increase supervision, and add unpredictability","Only increase the sample sizes used for specific substantive tests","Eliminate all analytical procedures from the planned audit approach","Reduce the total number of assertions tested across account balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330.05, when risks are assessed at the financial statement level, the auditor designs overall responses addressing the audit as a whole. AU-C 330.A1 identifies appropriate responses such as assigning more experienced staff, increasing supervision, and incorporating unpredictability into procedure selection.
Wrong (B): Increasing sample sizes for specific accounts addresses assertion-level risk, not pervasive financial statement-level risk requiring an overall audit response.
Wrong (C): Eliminating analytical procedures reduces the auditor''s evidence base and is the opposite of an appropriate response to elevated risk.
Wrong (D): Reducing the number of assertions tested when pervasive risks exist would increase the likelihood of failing to detect material misstatements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1503;

-- Q1505: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the client''s explanation for the identified control deficiency","Withdraw from the engagement due to the control weakness","Issue a qualified opinion on the entity''s internal controls","Increase assessed control risk and design substantive procedures accordingly"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330.07, when controls are not operating effectively, the auditor increases the assessed level of control risk and modifies planned substantive procedures to compensate. AU-C 330.A14 indicates the auditor may increase sample sizes, test closer to year-end, or change procedure types.
Wrong (A): Accepting the client''s explanation without modifying the audit approach would result in insufficient evidence when controls have been found ineffective.
Wrong (B): Withdrawing from the engagement is premature; the auditor should first design responsive substantive procedures to address the control weakness.
Wrong (C): Issuing a qualified opinion on internal controls is a reporting decision made after completing fieldwork, not an immediate response to finding a control deficiency.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1505;

-- Q1506: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Routine monthly depreciation entries recorded by the accounting staff","Standard payroll accrual entries processed through the normal cycle","Non-standard period-end entries with unusual account combinations","Recurring monthly entries for fixed operating expenses like rent"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 240.32, the auditor must test journal entries for evidence of management override. The focus is on non-standard entries, particularly those made near period-end, entries with unusual account combinations, entries made by unexpected individuals, or entries with round-dollar amounts.
Wrong (A): Routine depreciation entries are standard, recurring, and processed through normal controls, making them low-risk for management override.
Wrong (B): Standard payroll accruals follow established patterns and are typically subject to routine controls, posing minimal override risk.
Wrong (D): Recurring fixed-amount entries like rent follow predictable patterns and are unlikely vehicles for management override of controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1506;

-- Q1509: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Excluding goodwill from audit scope because it involves estimation","Only performing analytical procedures on the recorded goodwill balance","Evaluating controls over the risk and performing responsive substantive procedures","Accepting management''s goodwill impairment analysis without further testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330.15, for significant risks the auditor must obtain an understanding of the entity''s controls relevant to the risk and perform substantive procedures specifically responsive to the identified risk. For goodwill, AU-C 540.13 requires evaluating management''s impairment analysis and testing key assumptions.
Wrong (A): Excluding a significant risk area from audit scope violates AU-C 330.15, which requires specific substantive procedures for all identified significant risks.
Wrong (B): Performing only analytical procedures is insufficient for a significant risk; AU-C 330.21 requires substantive procedures that are specifically responsive to the nature of the risk.
Wrong (D): Accepting management''s analysis without independent evaluation provides insufficient evidence for a significant risk under AU-C 330.15 and AU-C 540.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1509;

-- Q1510: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the breach''s impact on data integrity and assess additional risks","Ignore the breach because cybersecurity is an IT issue, not an audit matter","Rely solely on management''s own assessment of the breach''s impact","Immediately withdraw from the engagement without further evaluation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.11–.12, the auditor must identify and assess risks by understanding the entity and its environment, including events affecting financial statements. A cybersecurity breach could compromise data integrity, introduce unauthorized transactions, or impair automated controls. AU-C 315.A30 requires considering such business risks.
Wrong (B): Cybersecurity breaches affecting financial reporting systems directly impact the reliability of financial data and controls, making them a core audit concern under AU-C 315.
Wrong (C): Relying solely on management''s assessment fails to satisfy the auditor''s independent obligation to evaluate risks of material misstatement under AU-C 315.
Wrong (D): Withdrawing is premature without first evaluating the severity and financial reporting implications of the breach to determine whether adequate procedures can be designed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1510;

-- Q1511: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the transaction was at arm''s length and could cause misstatement","Only whether the transaction is properly disclosed in the footnotes","Whether the related party entity is a publicly traded company","Only the dollar amount of the transaction without further analysis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 550.18, when assessing risk for related party transactions, the auditor should consider whether the transaction was conducted at arm''s length, the business purpose and economic substance, and whether the terms could result in material misstatement. AU-C 550.A27 notes that transactions outside normal business course present higher risk.
Wrong (B): Evaluating only footnote disclosure ignores the substance of the transaction; the auditor must assess whether non-arm''s-length terms could cause material misstatement.
Wrong (C): Whether the related party is publicly traded is irrelevant to assessing the risk of material misstatement arising from the terms and substance of the transaction.
Wrong (D): Evaluating only the dollar amount without considering the nature, purpose, and terms of the transaction fails to satisfy risk assessment obligations under AU-C 550.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1511;

-- Q1512: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue a scope limitation for the accounts receivable balance","Eliminate all substantive procedures for the accounts receivable area","Set detection risk low by performing more extensive substantive procedures","Accept a higher level of detection risk for accounts receivable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330.07, the audit risk model establishes that when the combined risk of material misstatement is high, the auditor must set detection risk at a low level to maintain acceptable audit risk. AU-C 330.A18 explains this means performing more persuasive procedures such as larger samples or testing closer to year-end.
Wrong (A): A scope limitation is a reporting matter resulting from inability to obtain evidence, not an appropriate planned response to high assessed risk of material misstatement.
Wrong (B): Eliminating substantive procedures when risk is high would make it impossible to reduce detection risk, directly contradicting the audit risk model.
Wrong (D): Accepting higher detection risk when the risk of material misstatement is already high would result in unacceptably high overall audit risk under AU-C 200.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1512;
